@node Code Organization, Creating Puzzle Assets, , Top
@chapter Code Organization

As with all Android projects, the @file{AndroidManifest.xml} file
under @file{app/src/main/} describes the main components of the
application (its activities).  Some of the specifics such as the
version number and supported SDK range are contained in
@file{app/build.gradle} instead of the manifest.

All code is under the top-level package
@code{com.xmission@/.trevin@/.android@/.tangram}.  The sub-packages
are:

@table @code
@item .data
Java objects that have little or no dependencies on Android

@item .exception
Custom exceptions used for reporting invalid puzzle definitions

@item .ui
Classes that implement the user interface of the application

@item .util
Utility classes; helper functions
@end table

The puzzles that are bundled with the application are located in
@file{app/src/main/assets/}.  All other resources used by the
application (UI layout, language-dependent strings, icons, etc.) are
under @file{app/src/main/res/}.

@node Data Classes, UI Classes, Code Organization, Code Organization
@section Data Classes

Following is an overview of the high-level Java objects used in
Tangram.

@table @code
@item TangramPuzzle
This is the basic container for puzzles.  It includes their name,
source, list of @code{TangramPiece}s, and methods for reading,
building, and validating puzzles.

@item TangramPiece
An abstract class representing a single tan.  It defines its shape,
rotation, position, and in the case of the parallelogram whether it is
flipped over.  It includes a few helper methods for checking whether
the edges of two tans are touching.  There are subclasses of this for
each type of tan.

@item TPoint
@c texi2pdf is incapable of emitting Unicode letterlike symbols like ℚ. :(
An @math{x, y} coordinate in @math{Q\sqrt{2}} space, which is known as
the @url{https://mathworld.wolfram.com/QuadraticField.html,Real
Quadratic Field}.  Coordinates are defined as
@math{a\sub{x}+b\sub{x}\sqrt{2}}, @math{a\sub{y}+b\sub{y}\sqrt{2}}.
Since most puzzles have tans oriented at multiples of 45@textdegree{},
using separate coefficients for the rational part and a multiple of
@math{\sqrt{2}} allows them to be integers in the vast majority of
cases.  This is an abstract class, with separate
@code{ImmutableTPoint} and @code{MutableTPoint} implementations that
are alternately used depending on whether ease of math operations or
reducing Java object allocations is needed more.

@item TEdge
A line segment in @math{Q\sqrt{2}} space.  This utility class is used
to help snap tans together, test for overlaps, and is a component of
@code{TPolygon}s.

@item TPolygon
A polygon in @math{Q\sqrt{2}} space.  This is used to find the
silhouette of a puzzle and compare two puzzles to see whether they are
identical (i.e. make the same shape).
@end table

@node UI Classes, Creating Puzzle Assets, Data Classes, Code Organization
@section UI Classes

Following are just a few of the UI components which are specialized
for the Tangram application.

@table @code
@item PieceTrayLayout
A custom layout which holds tans not yet placed on the playing field.
This contains operations for dragging tans to and from the playing
field, keeping track of how many pieces are available.

@item PlayTableView
The main play area where puzzles can be built.  This contains most of
the code for drawing the tans in play, moving them as the user drags
within a tan boundary, rotating them as the user moves them with two
fingers, moving the play field when the user drags outside of any
tans, and zooming in and out of the play field when the user pinches
or stretches it with two fingers.

@item TangramPuzzleView
A custom view that displays a static Tangram puzzle.  It contains
mostly the same drawing code as @code{PlayTableView}, but none of the
interactions.  This is used both to display the goal in the corner
when working on a puzzle and also to show previews of puzzles when
browsing the library.
@end table

@node Creating Puzzle Assets, Building from the Source, Code Organization, Top
@chapter Creating Puzzle Assets

Many puzzles can simply be created in the app.  When a valid tangram
is made in free-form mode, it can be saved to a folder that has been
configured in the application Preferences.  The program does it best
to ensure the puzzle is centered and aligned so that the tans'
positions use the simplest form, and for many puzzle where the tans
are all oriented in multiples of 45@textdegree{} and lined up at
their vertices the resulting puzzle definition can be used without
modification.  To add these puzzles to the program assets, copy the
user puzzle file from your emulator or device to the
@file{app/@/src/@/main/@/assets/} folder in your development
environment.

Some puzzles may require manual tweaking, especially if their edges
don't line up at the vertices or they are rotated at offsets of
15@textdegree{} or 30@textdegree{} from the normal 45@textdegree{}
angles.  For these cases I've used a vector drawing program with
templates of the tans laid out on a grid, and each tan has grid lines
and bisecting lines drawn through it to help measure out distances
between them.  Then measurements are taken by counting out grid points
for each coefficient: orthogonal lines count for the @math{a}
coefficient, and diagonals count for the @math{b} coefficient.  For
reference, here are the measurements for the tans in puzzle units:

@table @asis
@item Large Triangle
The long edge is 24 units wide, and the two short edges
@math{12\sqrt{2}} units long.  Its centroid is 4 units from the bottom
edge and 8 units from the right vertex, @math{4\sqrt{2}} units from
each short edge and @math{8\sqrt{2}} units from the acute vertices.

@item Medium Triangle
The long edge is @math{12\sqrt{2}} units wide, and the two short edges
12 units long.  Its centroid is @math{2\sqrt{2}} units from the bottom
edge and @math{4\sqrt{2}} units from the right vertex, 4 units from
each short edge and 8 units from the acute vertices.

@item Small Triangle
The long edge is 12 units wide, and the two short edges
@math{6\sqrt{2}} units long.  Its centroid is 2 units from the bottom
edge and 4 units from the right vertex, @math{2\sqrt{2}} units from
each short edge and @math{4\sqrt{2}} units from the acute vertices.

@item Square
The edges are @math{6\sqrt{2}} units long.  Its centroid is
@math{3\sqrt{2}} units from the edges and 6 units from the vertices.

@item Parallelogram
The long edges are 12 units long and the short edges @math{6\sqrt{2}}.
Its centroid is 3 units from the long edges, 6 units from the short
edges, @math{3\sqrt{2}} units from the obtuse vertices, and
@math{3\sqrt{10}} units from the acute vertices.
@end table

The coordinate system is oriented with @math{x} going from left
(negative) to right (positive), and @math{y} going from top (negative)
to bottom (positive).  Rotation is therefore defined with positive
values going clockwise.  Due to the use of @math{Q\sqrt{2}}
coefficients rotating tans by 45@textdegree{} involves simple addition
and division while swapping the coefficients:

@iftex
@tex
$$a_x^\prime = b_x - b_y$$
$$b_x^\prime = \frac{a_x}{2} - \frac{a_y}{2}$$
$$a_y^\prime = b_x + b_y$$
$$b_y^\prime = \frac{a_x}{2} + \frac{a_y}{2}$$
@end tex
@end iftex
@ifnottex
@table @asis
@item
@math{a\sub{x}\sup{\prime} = b\sub{x} - b\sub{y}}

@item
@math{b\sub{x}\sup{\prime} = \frac{a\sub{x}}{2} - \frac{a\sub{y}}{2}}

@item
@math{a\sub{y}\sup{\prime} = b\sub{x} + b\sub{y}}

@item
@math{b\sub{y}\sup{\prime} = \frac{a\sub{x}}{2} + \frac{a\sub{y}}{2}}
@end table
@end ifnottex

For other angles, it is recommended to start with the coefficients
best lined up to the nearest 90@textdegree{} angle at the point of
rotation and then apply the standard trigonometric transformations to
the relative coordinate of the centroid:

@iftex
@tex
$$x^\prime = x \cdot cos(\theta) - y \cdot sin(\theta)$$
$$y^\prime = x \cdot sin(\theta) + y \cdot cos(\theta)$$
@end tex
@end iftex
@ifnottex
@table @asis
@item
@math{x\sup{\prime} = x \cdot cos(\theta) - y \cdot sin(\theta)}

@item
@math{y\sup{\prime} = x \cdot sin(\theta) + y \cdot cos(\theta)}
@end table
@end ifnottex

If the resulting rotation ends up changing the width or height of the
tangram such that it is not in integer @math{Q\sqrt{2}} space, you may
want to add a bit of padding to one side to make the majority of tans
line up.  Several of the puzzles bundled with this app include an
extra @code{size_comment} field describing their alignments.

