@node Development Environment, Testing, Building from the Source, Building from the Source
@section Development Environment

Building the application @file{.apk} has been done using:
@itemize @bullet

@item Java 21 (bundled with Android Studio)

@item Android Studio Quail (2026.1.1), downloaded from the official
@url{https://developer.android.com/studio/,Android Studio} web site.

@item Gradle 9.4.1, installed by running
@code{./gradlew  wrapper --gradle-version=9.4.1}.

@item The Android Gradle Plugin version 9.2.1, as specified in the file
@file{gradle/libs.versions.toml}.

@end itemize

See @uref{http://developer.android.com/sdk/index.html} for information
on how to set up a project using the Android SDK.

@node Testing, Generating the Application Package, Generating Icons, Building from the Source
@section Testing

There are a variety of automated tests both stand-alone and
instrumented for testing on emulators.  These are in the
@file{app/src/test} and @file{app/src/androidTest} directories
respectively.

Running the stand-alone tests can be done from the command line:

@example
./gradlew :app:testDebugUnitTest
@end example

For the instrumented tests, it's recommended that you run these in
Android Studio where you can manage the virtual devices that the tests
run on.  At the moment the only instrumented tests are those which
check the puzzle asset files; these do not have any dependencies on
SDK features other than reading the assets, so it should only be
necessary to run them on a device with the minimum SDK level (23) and
target SDK (37).

@node Generating the Application Package, Generating Documentation, Testing, Building from the Source
@section Generating the Application Package (APK)

In Android Studio, click on the Build menu then ``Build APK''.  If
there were no errors, this should produce
@file{app/release/app-release.apk} (in Android Studio 2024+).  You
should rename this file to a more descriptive name like
@file{tangram-1.0.0.apk}.

Alternatively from the command line, run the following commands:

@example
./gradlew assembleRelease
@end example
This will produce an @emph{unsigned} APK in
@file{app/build/@/outputs/apk/@/release/@/app-release-unsigned.apk}.
To sign this, you will need to locate the build tools for the version
specified in @file{app/build.gradle} under
@code{android.buildToolsVersion} (if present; otherwise the most
recent build tools supported by the Android Gradle plugin).  You will
also need to know where the keystore of your app signing key is
located.
@verbatim
${BUILD_TOOLS_VERSION_DIRECTORY}/apksigner sign \
  --alignment-preserved \
  --ks ${ANDROID_KEYSTORE} --ks-key-alias ${SIGNING_KEY_ALIAS} \
  --out app/${APP_NAME}-${VERSION}.apk \
  app/build/outputs/apk/release/app-release-unsigned.apk
@end verbatim

@emph{(The @code{--alignment-preserved} option may be needed if you
are using build tools 35 or higher.)}

@node Generating Documentation,  , Generating the Application Package, Building from the Source
@section Generating Documentation

Lastly, if you need to generate a new edition of this manual or the
user manual, you will need
@uref{http://www.gnu.org/software/texinfo/,@command{texinfo}} and
@uref{https://www.gnu.org/software/texinfo/manual/texinfo/html_node/Format-with-texi2dvi-or-texi2pdf.html,@command{texi2pdf}}
and/or @uref{http://www.nongnu.org/texi2html/,@command{texi2html}}.
If you have @command{make} there is a @file{Makefile} which will make
running @code{texinfo} more convenient.

To generate the manuals in PDF, simply run:

@example
make pdf
@end example

if you have @command{make}, or:

@example
texi2pdf Building_Tangram.texinfo
texi2pdf Tangram.texinfo
@end example

To generate the manuals in HTML, run:

@example
make html
@end example

or:

@example
texi2html --split node Building_Tangram.texinfo
texi2html --split node Tangram.texinfo
@end example
@noindent
which will produce the manuals under
@file{doc/Building_Tangram/Building_Tangram.html} and
@file{doc/Tangram/Tangram.html}.

